        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Gas is faster on large flat concrete because it moves more water, and that is the whole of its advantage. Entry-level gas produces roughly 7,440 cleaning units against 6,000 for the strongest electric machines, a real gap of about a quarter that matters on a driveway and is invisible on a fence.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Everywhere else the electric machine is the better tool. Siding, decks, vehicles and furniture all want less pressure than gas delivers, so its extra output is unusable, while its noise, weight, fuel routine and exhaust are costs you pay on every job. A quiet induction machine also gets used for the five-minute tasks that a gas machine never gets dragged out for.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Decide with one number: how many square feet of flat concrete you clean, and how often. Below a couple of thousand square feet a year, buy a good electric machine and add a surface cleaner. Well above that on a regular schedule, buy gas, or keep the electric machine and rent gas for the one day a year it genuinely earns its keep.
          </p>
        </div>


